## What Are Gel Nails? (And Why Prom-Goers Love Them)

Gel nails is an umbrella term covering a few different systems, and knowing which one you're getting matters. A standard gel manicure means gel polish applied to your natural nail (or a short tip) and cured under a UV/LED lamp. No extension, no added length, but a chip-resistant, high-gloss finish that holds for up to two weeks.

Then there's Gel-X and similar soft gel extension systems. These use a pre-shaped soft gel tip adhered to your natural nail and cured under lamp, giving you length without the acrylic chemistry. BIAB (Builder In A Bottle) sits somewhere in between: a thicker gel that strengthens and extends the natural nail with a more flexible feel.

Gel-X deserves a specific callout for prom. It is the middle-ground option that most acrylic-hesitant first-timers do not know about. You get the length, skip the monomer smell, and the soak-off removal is gentler on the nail plate. The <a href='https://health.clevelandclinic.org/are-acrylic-nails-bad-for-you' target='_blank' rel='noopener'>Cleveland Clinic on acrylic nail risks</a> notes that traditional acrylic compounds carry a higher sensitivity risk than gel alternatives, relevant if you have reactive skin or have never had enhancements before.

Gel extensions do not match the rigidity of acrylics at extreme lengths. For medium-length prom nails, though, gel is a compelling and lower-risk choice.

